<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Ecology Is Changing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Summarize arguments from general and academic articles. Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize and evaluate arguments in articles about local governance and civic engagement, focusing on diverse viewpoints (e.g., "This article highlights that participation in town meetings can lead to better street repairs, but others argue that online surveys could be more inclusive.") Can analyze authentic texts about environmental restoration activities, evaluating their impact on the environment (e.g., "Planting trees along the river has improved air quality and reduced erosion.") Can extract main points from articles on forest health and share them in reviews, noting environmental improvements (e.g., "Our community's recycling program has successfully reduced waste in local landfills.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two contrasting images on the board: one of a lush, healthy forest and another of an area affected by deforestation or pollution. Students work in pairs to brainstorm 3-4 adjectives to describe each image. Teacher then elicits ideas, asking, "What differences do you observe? What words come to mind when you see these images?" (e.g., 'healthy,' 'dramatic,' 'polluted,' 'sustainable'). This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to the theme "How Our Ecology Is Changing" and prepares them for the reading task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly states the lesson's focus on reading and how to approach the upcoming text. Teacher models skimming a text for general understanding by quickly glancing at titles, headings, and images. Teacher then models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on the /aɪ/ sound in words like 'life,' 'biodiversity,' and 'type,' and the reduced /ɪt/ in 'climate' and 'accurate.' Teacher uses the words in simple sentences related to the scenario, e.g., "Forests are full of diverse marine life."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms that will appear in the reading.
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'deforestation' when we plant more trees or cut them down?" (Expected: Cut them down.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a forest has 'biodiversity,' does it have many different species or just one?" (Expected: Many different species.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> Students will read an authentic non-fiction article titled "Panama's Forests: A Call for Conservation" which discusses the impact of deforestation, the importance of biodiversity, and current conservation efforts, including community engagement and restoration activities. The article will implicitly contain examples of present perfect, first/second conditionals, and comparatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An excerpt (approx. 300-350 words) from an online environmental magazine or local NGO report, formatted as a blog post or news article. The register is semi-formal and informative, focusing on current ecological changes and conservation actions in Panama.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend main ideas and specific details from authentic non-fiction texts about environmental issues, evaluating arguments and understanding viewpoints.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article once quickly and choose the best title from three options provided by the teacher (e.g., "The History of Panama," "Forests Under Threat and Solutions," "How to Plant a Tree"). This checks overall understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again more carefully and determine whether 5-6 statements related to the text are true or false, correcting the false ones. (e.g., "Deforestation has not significantly affected biodiversity in Panama." "Planting trees along rivers has improved air quality.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match specific environmental challenges (e.g., pollution, logging) mentioned in the article to their described impacts (e.g., 'reduced marine life,' 'loss of native species').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on identifying and using target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, conservation, impact, reduce, improve, protect, recycle) and grammar (present perfect, first and second conditionals, comparatives) within the context of the article to enhance reading comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary Cloze:</b> The teacher provides a paragraph derived from the article with 5-6 blanks. Students complete the blanks using target vocabulary words from a given list (e.g., deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, conservation, improve, affect). This reinforces vocabulary in context.
                        <br>Example: "Over the last decade, _____ has significantly _____ local _____ near our rivers. However, recent _____ efforts aim to _____ air quality."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar Identification & Sentence Completion:</b> Students re-read specific sentences or paragraphs from the article.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They identify and underline examples of present perfect, first conditional, second conditional, and comparatives.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, students complete 3-4 sentence stems based on the article, using the target grammar.
                                <br>Example: "If we (plant) more trees, (we/help/reduce) deforestation." (First Conditional)
                                <br>Example: "Deforestation (affect/significantly) biodiversity in our area for years." (Present Perfect)
                                <br>Example: "Urban ecosystems have (less/many) biodiversity compared to natural habitats." (Comparatives)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check and Discussion:</b> In pairs, students compare their answers for the cloze activity and sentence completion. They discuss *why* certain words or grammatical structures are correct, referring back to the article to justify their choices. Teacher monitors, listening for accurate use of vocabulary and grammar and facilitating discussion using pragmatic language (e.g., "How has deforestation affected our area, according to the text?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Article Review & Recommendations. Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to create a short, informal "review" of the article they read, focusing on summarizing its main arguments and discussing potential local actions. They will then orally share their review and recommendations with another group or the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses the article and identifies the 2-3 most important arguments or facts presented about "Our Changing Forests."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then brainstorm 2-3 practical, local conservation ideas or environmental improvements mentioned or inspired by the article. (e.g., "If we recycle more, we can reduce plastic waste in landfills and protect marine life.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups prepare a short (2-3 minute) oral summary and recommendation, making sure to use vocabulary like 'deforestation,' 'biodiversity,' 'conservation,' 'impact,' 'improve,' and grammatical structures such as present perfect ("Deforestation has dramatically affected...") and conditionals ("If we protect our forests, we will maintain healthier ecosystems."). They should also practice polite expressions for environmental concerns ("Protecting our forests benefits everyone.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their review to another group or the class, responding to questions about their analysis.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and presentations, focusing on students' ability to summarize arguments and apply target language. A quick written exit ticket will also be collected.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually write a short paragraph (3-5 sentences) summarizing one main argument from the article and one environmental improvement that has been achieved or could be achieved (e.g., "Our community's recycling program has successfully reduced waste in local landfills."). They should use at least two target vocabulary words and one target grammatical structure (present perfect or conditional).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in summarizing a main argument from the article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., deforestation, conservation, impact, improve).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of at least one target grammatical structure (present perfect, conditionals, comparatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and cohesion of the written paragraph.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about forest health from the article is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The most challenging part of summarizing the article was..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel in summarizing information from non-fiction articles about environmental issues?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which vocabulary words or grammar structures from today's lesson will be most useful for me when discussing environmental impact?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the reading activities? Students were highly engaged with the authentic text and the collaborative summary task fostered good communication. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with differentiating between first and second conditionals; more targeted practice will be needed. For Lesson 3 (Speaking), I will ensure the speaking prompts directly encourage the use of these structures based on the arguments summarized today. I also noticed that some students need more practice in evaluating diverse viewpoints; I will integrate a task that explicitly asks them to consider different perspectives in our next speaking activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3, students will actively use the vocabulary and arguments from this reading lesson to engage in speaking and interaction tasks. They will discuss the environmental impacts and conservation ideas presented in the article, applying the present perfect, conditionals, and comparatives in spoken dialogues and debates related to "Our Changing Forests."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>